1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.203
Welcome to A2A, the Agent2Agent protocol,

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:06.172
built in partnership with Google Cloud and IBM Research.

3
00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.707
In this course, you'll build and

4
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.476
run agents that communicate through the A2A

5
00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.478
or Agent2Agent protocol.

6
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.547
There used to be multiple competing protocols

7
00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.850
for agent-to-agent communication.

8
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.252
But since IBM and Google joined forces,

9
00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.956
A2A is emerging as the leading industry standard.

10
00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:27.627
In this course, you configure sequential and hierarchical workflows

11
00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:32.332
of multiple agents that collaborate through a unified interface provided by A2A.

12
00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.267
I'm delighted that the instructors for this

13
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.236
course are Holt Skinner and Ivan Nardini

14
00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:39.839
from Google Cloud, and returning instructor Sandi Besen from

15
00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:43.643
IBM. Thanks Andrew. We're excited to work with you on this course.

16
00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.446
To build a team of agents that collaborate through A2A,

17
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.749
you can wrap your agent in an A2A server and publish

18
00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.317
an agent card, which is a

19
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.053
JSON document that describes the agent's capabilities,

20
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.056
endpoint, and authentication requirements.

21
00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:00.160
an A2A client, which can itself be part of another AI agent

22
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.362
or any other software application,

23
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:06.166
then discovers the A2A server and reads that contract

24
00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:09.169
to determine how to structure requests to the agent to execute.

25
00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:12.272
This standardized approach helps teams

26
00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:15.675
build agents independently and connect them seamlessly.

27
00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:20.580
Take for example, a multi-agent system for customer support.

28
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.083
Maybe one team will build a logistics agent

29
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.185
to answer questions related to order status,

30
00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.487
and a different team might build a RAG agent

31
00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:30.090
to answer general questions about the products.

32
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:34.094
Each of these agents could even be built with a different framework

33
00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.796
and then exposed to an A2A server.

34
00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:39.165
An orchestrator agent

35
00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:42.068
can process customer inquiries and route them

36
00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:43.870
to the specialized agent using A2A.

37
00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.873
Since the communication happens

38
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:48.775
through a standardized client-server interface,

39
00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.244
each team can update their agents

40
00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.947
without the others needing to also change their code.

41
00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.050
This standardization also makes it easy to

42
00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.520
integrate existing agents into a multi-agent workflow.

43
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.623
Many frameworks like Google Agent Development Kit,

44
00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.292
Microsoft Agent Framework and BeeAI

45
00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:08.394
have built-in A2A support,

46
00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:11.397
making it simple to expose or connect

47
00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:14.400
to A2A agents. A2A is an open source

48
00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.403
and community governed under the Linux Foundation.

49
00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.840
Google launched A2A in April 2025,

50
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.612
and IBM's agent communication protocol joined forces with A2A shortly after.

51
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.915
And while A2A is decoupled from MCP,

52
00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:32.218
A2A and MCP are complementary protocols.

53
00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:35.622
MCP standardized how an agent connects to its tools,

54
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.725
APIs, and resources to get information.

55
00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:42.829
A2A standardized how an agent connects to peer agents.

56
00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:46.366
In this course you'll build a healthcare multi-agent system

57
00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.368
consisting of three remote agents.

58
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.070
an insurance agent to answer coverage questions

59
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.072
using Claude on Vertex AI.

60
00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.676
a health research agent to search for health-related information

61
00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.678
using Google Agent Development Kit

62
00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:01.681
and a doctor matching agent to find doctors using LangGraph.

63
00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:05.084
You'll learn how to wrap each agent into an A2A server

64
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.687
and how to build a simple A2A client

65
00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:09.889
that you can use to communicate to any A2A server.

66
00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.126
You'll also see how to connect to A2A servers

67
00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:16.429
using clients from Microsoft Agent Framework and Google ADK.

68
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.032
You'll then use Google Agent Development Kit

69
00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:21.534
to chain the insurance and research agents sequentially

70
00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.369
and connect the doctor matching agent

71
00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.338
to an MCP server for doctor data.

72
00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.208
After that, you'll orchestrate all three agents

73
00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.511
into an agentic system using the BeeAI framework.

74
00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.913
Instead of hard coding and chaining the agents,

75
00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.716
you'll dynamically hand off tasks to specialized agents

76
00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.619
using the requirement agent in the BeeAI framework.

77
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.021
And finally, you'll learn how to deploy, run,

78
00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.624
and import A2A agents using Agent Stack,

79
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.527
which is open source, self-hostable infrastructure

80
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:50.430
for teams needing fast deployment of agents,

81
00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.732
framework flexibility and data control.

82
00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:55.034
Many people have worked to create this course.

83
00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.538
I'd like to thank the A2A Technical Steering Committee

84
00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.941
from IBM Research, the BeeAI and Agent Stack team,

85
00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:04.244
and from DeepLearning.AI, Hawraa Salami.

86
00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:07.447
A2A standardizes communication between agents that

87
00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:09.148
could be built by the same developer,

88
00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:12.151
by different teams within the same organization

89
00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:15.355
or even by teams across different organizations.

90
00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:19.158
It helps us to standardize multi-agent collaboration,

91
00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:20.793
and that's what you'll learn more

92
00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.462
about in the first lesson with Holt.

93
00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.031
Let's go to the next video to get started.
